HIGHLY RIGHT-SKEWED. On the as-supplied linear scale the RNA expression values are highly right-skewed: pooled Fisher-Pearson moment skewness G1 = 42.4 over all 6,724,656 gene-by-sample values (mean 932 vs median 177; max 1,336,107 = 7,558x the median), and all five robust/bounded estimators agree on the direction (Bowley quartile +0.58, 10/50/90 percentile +0.83, 5/50/95 percentile +0.90, Pearson median +0.57, 5%-trimmed G1 +1.93 - the trimmed value alone already exceeds the |1| 'highly' cut-off, so the verdict does not depend on outliers). All three units of observation give the same pre-registered class - pooled G1 = 42.4; median per-sample G1 = 20.63 (IQR 17.9-26.5, 100% of samples right-skewed); median per-gene G1 = 2.03 (IQR 1.14-4.42, 99.8% of genes right-skewed) - as does every sensitivity population (tumour-only G1 = 36.0, drop-all-zero-genes 42.0, drop >=50%-zero genes 39.6, deduplicated symbols 42.4; all 'highly right-skewed'). Interpretation: the magnitude is dominated by a long upper tail - the top 1% of values contribute 99.8% of the third central moment and 27.7% of total expression - but the rightward asymmetry is not an outlier artefact: dropping that top 1% still leaves G1 = 3.6, and the quantile-based estimators (which the extreme tail cannot move) stay firmly positive. Shape evidence for the asymmetry independent of the moment: 77% of values lie below the mean; the median-to-upper ratios are p90/p50 = 11.5 and p99/p50 = 61.5 (mirror ratios p50/p10, p50/p01 are bounded by the zero floor); and the maximum sits 338 SD above the mean, versus <6 SD for any Gaussian sample of this size. Note the one genuine magnitude difference between views: within-gene distributions across the 328 samples are only mildly right-skewed (median Bowley +0.17), whereas the pooled and per-sample views are extreme; the direction is nevertheless identical everywhere. Finally, on the secondary log2(x+1) scale the SAME data become approximately normal (G1 = -0.30; nonzero-only minimally left-skewed, G1 = -0.50), i.e. the values are approximately log-normal - so the asymmetry is a property of the natural (linear) scale and is removed by log-transforming; the 15.2% exact-zero floor is not the driver (removing all zeros still gives G1 = 39.5). Answer key: HIGHLY RIGHT-SKEWED.

